WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.790
- I don't feel like I'm
taking crazy pills anymore.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.710
The 11th Circuit has
ruled against Donald Trump

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.140
in the Mar-a-Lago
classified documents case,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.480
and it is a beat down.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.370
Now, because today is
a day that ends in Y,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.960
that means this channel is here to explain

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.460
what all of this means,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.980
because try as I might
to get away from Trump,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.300
he just keeps inserting himself

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.840
into the most interesting
legal cases in the country.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.100
So, strap in for an explanation

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.930
for why Judge Cannon might have ruled

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.550
in Trump's favor initially.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.880
The Court of Appeals is not
going to suffer any fools.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.080
So, let's start with a recap

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.460
of what brought us to this point.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.770
On September 5th, 2022,
Judge Aileen Cannon,

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.330
who was controversially
confirmed to the bench

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.400
after Trump was defeated for reelection,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.200
authorized the appointment
of a special master

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.836
to conduct an independent
review of some 11,000 documents

00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:46.050
and some 1,800 items that
were seized from Mar-a-Lago

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.940
pursuant to a search warrant.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.650
Judge Cannon also took
the extraordinary step

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.120
of temporarily enjoining the government

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.130
from reviewing and using
the seized materials

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.540
for investigative purposes,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.610
pending completion of the
special master's review

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.410
or further order of the court.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.820
In other words, Judge Cannon's ruling

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.890
forced the DOJ to halt
its criminal investigation

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.280
of Donald Trump until the special
master completed his work.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.113
Though she did allow

00:01:06.113 --> 00:01:07.830
the Office of the Director
of National Intelligence,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.920
the ODNI, to continue
its classification review

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.680
and/or intelligence
assessment of those documents.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.736
Now, the DOJ swiftly appealed
Judge Cannon's ruling

00:01:15.736 --> 00:01:16.920
to the 11th Circuit.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.830
That is the Court of Appeals
that oversees Judge Cannon.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.870
But it also separately
petitioned Judge Cannon

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.640
for a partial stay of her ruling.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.270
It went back to her to
ask her to partially stay

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.770
part of the things that she had ordered.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.670
Now, in that motion,

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.530
the DOJ argued that
Judge Cannon's injunction

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.510
risked irreparable harm
to national security

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.940
by interfering with the
government's criminal investigation

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.250
and forcing disclosure of
these classified documents

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.360
to a special master.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.640
And the DOJ further argued
that it's simply not possible

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.040
to separate the DOJ and the
FBI's criminal investigation,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.060
which was blocked,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.950
from the intelligence
community's assessment,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.720
which was allowed to continue.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.950
As a result, the DOJ argued

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.380
that the order had created
confusion in the executive branch

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.903
and disrupted intelligence
agencies' ability

00:01:53.903 --> 00:01:56.730
to assess any damage to national security.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.560
For relief, the DOJ
requested a partial stay

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.850
of Judge Cannon's order

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.610
that would allow the FBI
to use the 103 documents

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.220
marked as classified for
further criminal investigation

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.620
and not require the disclosure
to the special master.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.850
And on September 15th,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.740
Judge Cannon rejected the DOJ's motion,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:11.880
and in her ruling,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.710
Judge Cannon refused to accept
the government's argument

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.420
that Trump could never
assert any privilege

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.190
or property interest on the documents

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.390
with classified markings.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.160
Those are property of the government.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.720
Judge Cannon also refused to accept

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.740
the government's contention

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.060
that the documents marked as classified

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.350
are actually classified,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.210
or contain extremely sensitive information

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.430
that could harm national
security if disclosed.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.800
Judge Cannon then formally
appointed Judge Raymond Dearie

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.650
to lead an independent review
of the seized materials.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.200
Then, after Trump and the
DOJ submitted two names

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.300
to be appointed as special master,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.120
Judge Cannon agreed to
appoint Judge Raymond Dearie,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.800
who was one of Trump's picks,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.110
to lead an independent review
of the seized material.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.229
She further directed Judge
Dearie to prioritize review

00:02:48.229 --> 00:02:51.330
of the roughly 100 documents
with classification markings,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.400
with a goal of completing the
review of all the documents

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.200
by November 30th, 2022.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.700
Now, we'll talk about this later,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.468
but Trump may live to regret

00:02:58.468 --> 00:03:00.720
this whole special master gambit,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.760
and especially regret agreeing to Dearie

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.080
as the special master.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.630
But the DOJ then appealed the
denial of the partial stay

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.070
to the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.256
So, let's talk about
this particular decision

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:12.120
from the 11th Circuit.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.390
Now, legal experts attacked
Judge Cannon's ruling en masse

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.420
as legally unsound and
ripe for being overturned.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.460
However, there was some
concern that the 11th Circuit

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.260
would let Judge Cannon's injunction stand.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.760
After all, the 11th Circuit

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.710
is a relatively conservative
court of appeals,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.980
with Trump having nominated
six of the 11 active judges

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.000
on the circuit.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.910
And recent history shows
how some Trump appointees,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.350
like Judge Cannon herself,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.320
have been quite unpredictable
in their particular rulings.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.247
But in a unanimous opinion
issued on September 21st,

00:03:40.247 --> 00:03:41.820
the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.580
granted the government's
motion for a partial stay.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.040
In a strongly-worded 29-page decision,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:47.940
the three-judge panel,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.090
made up of two Trump appointees
and one Obama appointee,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.730
ruled that Judge Cannon
likely erred as matter of law

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.200
by exercising jurisdiction

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.687
to "enjoin the United States's
use of classified records

00:03:58.687 --> 00:03:59.887
"in its criminal investigation

00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:01.057
"and to require the United States

00:04:01.057 --> 00:04:03.187
"to submit marked classified documents

00:04:03.187 --> 00:04:05.250
"to a special master for review."

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.565
Now, this decision was per curiam,

00:04:06.565 --> 00:04:10.260
meaning that all three judges
equally supported the opinion.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.060
And as a result, the Justice Department

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.590
was no longer prohibited

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.045
from investigating the documents

00:04:15.045 --> 00:04:18.240
marked with classified
markings that were seized

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.009
nor would it be required
to share those materials

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:22.560
to the special master for
his independent review.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.810
But it's not just that the
11th Circuit granted the DOJ

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.250
its requested relief.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.140
What's so notable about this opinion

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.954
is how it reads like a
complete repudiation,

00:04:29.954 --> 00:04:32.790
both the legal reasoning
behind Judge Cannon's order

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.880
and some of Trump's more
specious factual claims.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.190
Now, in deciding whether to
grant the stay pending appeal,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.110
the Court of Appeals
evaluated four factors

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.430
articulated by the Supreme Court

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.740
in the Nken versus Holder case.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.260
First, whether the stay applicant
has made a strong showing

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.270
that he is likely to
succeed on the merits.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.520
Two, whether the applicant
will be irreparably injured

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.600
absent a stay.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.860
Three, whether issuance of the stay

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.720
will substantially injure
the other party's interest

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:55.553
in the proceedings.

00:04:55.553 --> 00:04:57.960
And four, whether the
public interest lies.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.160
And on every single point,

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.810
the 11th Circuit sided with the DOJ

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.750
to find that the stay pending
the appeal was justified.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.583
Now, for the first factor,

00:05:04.583 --> 00:05:07.440
the DOJ demonstrated it was
likely to succeed on the merits

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.200
of its claim under the Richey analysis.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.390
Now, when a person seeks
return of seized property

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.620
in pre-indictment cases,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.230
those actions are governed
by equitable principles,

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.850
whether viewed as based on

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.890
Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 41(g)

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.470
or the general equitable
jurisdiction of federal courts.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.660
That's according to the Richey case.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.710
And under the Richey case,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.050
when evaluating whether to
intervene pre-indictment,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.390
courts assess four factors
pursuant to Richey,

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.040
which we talked about in our prior video.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.917
One, whether the government
displayed a callous disregard

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:36.780
for constitutional rights in
seizing the items at issue.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.510
Two, whether the plaintiff
has an individual interest in

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.690
and need for the material
whose return he seeks.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.000
Three, whether the plaintiff
would be irreparably injured

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.800
by denial of the return of property.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.260
And four, whether the plaintiff
has an adequate remedy

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.900
at law for the redress of his grievance.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.760
Now, under all four of the Richey factors,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.190
the Court of Appeal found that
the DOJ was likely to succeed

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.270
on the merits of its claim.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.890
Namely, that Judge Cannon's order

00:05:58.890 --> 00:05:59.918
blocking the criminal investigation

00:05:59.918 --> 00:06:02.700
and turning over classified
records to the special master

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.460
was an abusive discretion
that was made in error.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.530
Now, the panel noted that
even Judge Cannon found

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.780
no callous disregard for
Trump's constitutional rights

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.940
and neither party contested this finding.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.020
And the Court of Appeals pointed out

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.430
that this alone should have been enough

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.297
to end the district court's inquiry.

00:06:16.297 --> 00:06:18.277
"The absence of this indispensable factor

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.707
"in the Richey analysis is
reason enough to conclude

00:06:20.707 --> 00:06:22.357
:that the district court
abused its discretion

00:06:22.357 --> 00:06:24.810
"in exercising equitable
jurisdiction here."

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.190
Now, it's basically judicial lawyer speak

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.720
for telling Judge Cannon politely

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.450
that her entire analysis was
flawed from the very beginning.

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.310
Now, this is a factor-based test,

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:34.380
but basically the court
can almost never exercise

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.300
its equitable jurisdiction

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.506
without the presence of callous disregard

00:06:37.506 --> 00:06:39.330
for constitutional rights.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.430
And in this case, it wasn't even close.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.080
But for the sake of
doing a complete takedown

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.020
of Judge Cannon's opinion, the
Court of Appeals continued.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.390
Now, for example, while Judge
Cannon was open to Trump

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:49.950
having a potential property interest

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.150
in classified materials,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.290
the 11th Circuit was skeptical

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.997
that Trump had any property
interest in them at all.

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:57.247
"For our part, we cannot
discern why Plaintiff

00:06:57.247 --> 00:06:58.807
"would have an individual interest in

00:06:58.807 --> 00:07:01.087
"or need for any of the
one-hundred documents

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.670
"with classified markings."

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.080
Discussing the law of classification,

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.920
the court explained that access
to classified information

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.830
is only allowed if a person

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.210
has a need to know the information

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.040
or if the current administration

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.480
has waived this requirement.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.830
But as the Court of Appeals noted,

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.240
Trump hadn't provided any evidence

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.227
that either of these things had happened.

00:07:17.227 --> 00:07:18.847
"Plaintiff has not even attempted to show

00:07:18.847 --> 00:07:20.287
"that he has a need to
know the information

00:07:20.287 --> 00:07:22.057
"contained in the classified documents.

00:07:22.057 --> 00:07:24.037
"Nor has he established that
the current administration

00:07:24.037 --> 00:07:25.897
"has waived the requirement
for these documents."

00:07:25.897 --> 00:07:28.387
"And even if he had,
that, in and of itself,

00:07:28.387 --> 00:07:31.327
"would not explain why Plaintiff
has an individual interest

00:07:31.327 --> 00:07:33.120
"in the classified documents."

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.830
The Court of Appeals
called Trump's suggestion

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.140
that he declassified these
documents a red herring

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.000
because declassification
would have zero effect

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.730
on the character of the
document and would not explain

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.860
why he has a personal interest in them.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.262
As many legal experts have noted,

00:07:45.262 --> 00:07:46.990
Trump has repeatedly
claimed on social media

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:49.680
and in interviews that he
declassified the documents.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.745
But Trump specifically
avoided making these claims

00:07:51.745 --> 00:07:54.540
in a judicial setting supported by,

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:55.373
what's the word?

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.325
Evidence, that's it.

00:07:57.325 --> 00:07:59.068
No evidence whatsoever.

00:07:59.068 --> 00:08:01.267
And the Court of Appeals noticed.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:02.737
"The record contains no evidence

00:08:02.737 --> 00:08:04.747
"that any of these
records were declassified.

00:08:04.747 --> 00:08:05.947
"And before the special master,

00:08:05.947 --> 00:08:07.867
"Plaintiff resisted providing any evidence

00:08:07.867 --> 00:08:10.470
"that he had declassified
any of these documents."

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.210
Now, what the Court of
Appeals was referring to

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.342
was just days earlier,

00:08:13.342 --> 00:08:15.044
Trump's lawyers specifically refused

00:08:15.044 --> 00:08:17.176
the special master's
request to provide evidence

00:08:17.176 --> 00:08:19.410
that these documents were declassified,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.000
claiming that doing so would force Trump

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.257
to "disclose a defense to the merits

00:08:23.257 --> 00:08:24.870
"of any subsequent indictment."

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.700
And in fact, they're absolutely right,

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.040
and it would be entirely prejudicial

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.280
to disclose a defense to an indictment.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.530
But those are post-indictment arguments,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:35.940
not pre-indictment arguments,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.710
like what we're dealing with right here.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.540
But they should probably talk
to their client about that

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.600
because he keeps going
on TV and locking himself

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.580
into specific defenses

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.850
that actually are not
particularly good defenses at all.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:48.683
Go figure.

00:08:48.683 --> 00:08:50.190
But at any rate, regarding
the third Richey factor

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.720
of irreparable harm to Trump,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.210
Judge Cannon had argued that
he could be potentially harmed

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.190
if the classified documents
were not returned, based on:

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.617
one, improper disclosure
of sensitive information

00:08:58.617 --> 00:09:00.150
to the public;

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.520
two, the United States retention

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.360
and use of privileged materials;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.720
and three, the stigma associated
with future prosecution.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.690
But the Court of Appeals
explained in meticulous detail

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.420
why all of those
arguments were 100% wrong.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.040
For starters, the whole
reason the government

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.150
was investigating the documents

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.250
was to limit unauthorized disclosure,

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.170
and Trump has not asserted
attorney-client privilege

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.220
over the classified documents

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.560
so there can't be a harm to
his privilege there either.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.210
And as to the final Richey factor,

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.280
which asked whether the
plaintiff has an adequate remedy

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.170
at law for the redress of his grievance,

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.450
well, the court noted that
Trump has a remedy at law.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.910
He simply has chosen not to invoke it.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:34.320
The court explained that Trump could file

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.200
a Rule 41(g) motion to
get the documents back,

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.130
but he hasn't.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.030
But even if he did,

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.977
Trump "has not identified any reason

00:09:40.977 --> 00:09:43.980
"that he is entitled to those documents."

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.200
So, based on the four-factor Richey test,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.140
the DOJ demonstrated it was
likely to succeed on the merits.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:51.000
But returning to the Nken analysis,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:51.900
the Court of Appeals held

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.970
that the United States would
suffer irreparable harm

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.000
if Judge Cannon's injunction
was not partially stayed.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.691
Finding that the government's
national security review

00:09:58.691 --> 00:10:02.130
is inextricably intertwined
with its criminal investigation,

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.260
the 11th Circuit explained that
allowing the order to stand

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.300
would create a chilling
effect on national security.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.100
Basically, it's not
good for the government

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.310
when it cannot control
extremely classified documents.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.450
And to illustrate this,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.370
the court noted that officials who oversee

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:15.823
both national security and
criminal investigations

00:10:15.823 --> 00:10:17.640
into classified information

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.410
might be forced to choose
between doing their jobs

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.300
and facing contempt charges.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:22.710
And with respect to the third Nken factor,

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.900
the DOJ demonstrated irreparable harm

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.720
while Trump had not
demonstrated irreparable harm.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:28.560
The court reiterated

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.020
that Trump does not have
a possessory interest

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.100
in the documents at issue,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.260
so he does not suffer a cognizable harm

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.730
if the United States reviews the documents

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.700
he neither owns nor has
a personal interest in.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.140
Nor, the court affirmed,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.030
is being criminally investigated itself

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.577
a substantial injury to justify a stay.

00:10:45.577 --> 00:10:49.387
"Bearing the discomfiture and
cost of prosecution for crime

00:10:49.387 --> 00:10:50.737
"even by an innocent person

00:10:50.737 --> 00:10:53.580
"is one of the painful
obligations of citizenship."

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.830
That is, of course,
according to the famous

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.320
Supreme Court case of
Cobbledick versus United States.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.600
And finally, regarding the
public interest factor,

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.989
the court noted that
the classified documents

00:11:01.989 --> 00:11:03.937
contain information that

00:11:03.937 --> 00:11:05.677
"the unauthorized disclosure of

00:11:05.677 --> 00:11:07.537
"which reasonably could
be expected to cause

00:11:07.537 --> 00:11:10.200
"exceptionally grave damage
to the national security."

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.210
The public naturally has
an interest in ensuring

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:13.560
this does not happen.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.300
However, a national security assessment

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.855
can only be done if the
government is allowed

00:11:16.855 --> 00:11:18.420
to review these documents,

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.480
which under the current
injunction couldn't have happened.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.370
And having found the balance of equities

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.500
overwhelmingly favored the DOJ,

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.720
the Court of Appeals granted
the motion for partial stay

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.840
of Judge Cannon's order
pending the entire appeal.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.210
Now, essentially, the Court
of Appeals eviscerated

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.370
Cannon's order regarding
the classified documents

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.110
and all of its underlying logic.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.060
But here, the DOJ's
appeal was very narrow.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:40.590
Probably for strategic reasons,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:41.970
the DOJ only appealed the order

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.800
regarding classified documents.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.590
That basically narrowed the
focus of the Court of Appeals

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:49.980
on the classified documents
issue to ignore everything else.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:50.813
But the upshot, though,

00:11:50.813 --> 00:11:53.370
is that the rest of the
special master order remains,

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.260
which for reasons I'll
explain in just a moment,

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.030
is kind of hilarious at this point.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.620
But let's talk about what happened next

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.750
with Judge Cannon and the special master.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.090
Judge Cannon responded to the
partial stay the following day

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.670
by modifying her order
appointing the special master.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.410
Judge Cannon revised her original order

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.710
by excluding the 100-odd
documents with classified markings

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:12.330
from the special master's review,

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.190
deleting the requirement
that the special master

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.830
submit interim reports and
recommendations to the court

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.030
throughout its review,

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.085
and striking a provision
requiring classified documents

00:12:20.085 --> 00:12:23.100
under review to be made
available for Trump's counsel

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.740
for inspection under the
special master's supervision.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.830
And this might be the 11th
Circuit's last word on this issue

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.810
and it may never get to the Supreme Court

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.060
because according to many
respected legal analysts,

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:36.030
like Steve Vladeck at
the University of Texas,

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:37.710
Trump can't ask the entire 11th Circuit

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.960
to rehear the appeal on bunk

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.270
because the judge has
basically removed anything

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:43.530
that was objectionable.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.150
Same thing goes for the Supreme Court.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.430
And that same day, Special
Master Raymond Dearie

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:48.900
released a case management plan

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.510
for his review of the
Mar-a-Lago documents.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.430
Now, in this plan, he
asked Trump's legal team

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.990
to state by September 30th

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.553
whether they believed
any of the seized items

00:12:56.553 --> 00:12:58.050
were incorrectly described

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.870
by the Justice Department's
11-page inventory list.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:02.580
And the judge also
asked Trump's legal team

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:03.600
to state whether they claim

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.280
any of the items on the inventory list

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.710
were not in fact taken from the premises.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.510
Trump has repeatedly
claimed on social media

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.460
and in interviews that
the FBI planted evidence

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.350
when they searched Mar-a-Lago.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.560
Claims that his lawyers have
specifically not made in court,

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.240
probably because it requires evidence

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.250
and subjects you to sanctions.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.740
Instead, Trump's legal team
asserts that they can't affirm

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.360
that the government's
inventory is accurate

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.640
without first reviewing
the seized materials.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.910
But as to Trump's out-of-court
claims of planted evidence,

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:33.150
Judge Dearie is asking his
legal team to, in legal speak,

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:34.290
put up or shut up.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.018
And because Dearie is not putting up

00:13:36.018 --> 00:13:38.370
with some of the same
nonsense that Cannon did,

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.020
it seems like Trump may come to regret

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.120
pushing for this
particular special master.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.190
The impact of the 11th
Circuit's ruling was immediate

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:46.020
and not limited to Trump's case.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.810
The same day Judge Cannon
modified her order,

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.160
a Trump-appointed federal judge

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:51.450
cited the 11th Circuit's ruling

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:54.690
to deny Mike MyPillow Lindell's motion

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.630
for the return of his
cell phone by the FBI.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:58.920
Mike Lindell, who you probably know,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.530
has propagated increasingly
bizarre conspiracy theories

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:03.600
about how the 2020 election was stolen

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.210
and that he can get Donald
Trump reinstated as president,

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.070
is being investigated
by federal authorities

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:09.930
in connection with the breach

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.810
of a Colorado county's voting system.

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.300
His phone was seized at
a Hardee's drive-through,

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:16.560
which isn't legally relevant.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.570
It's just, I think, very, very funny.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.549
And bizarrely, hours after
the Court of Appeals ruling,

00:14:20.549 --> 00:14:22.200
Trump went on Sean Hannity

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.150
to vent about the
variety of legal troubles

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.080
that he's facing,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.670
like the New York Attorney General's

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:28.080
civil fraud action against Trump

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:30.990
and three of his adult
middle-aged children

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:33.030
that was announced earlier in the day.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.880
- I actually thought that
they would never bring a case

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:36.960
and she brought it.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.550
- And when the conversation turned

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:39.780
to the classified documents,

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.930
Trump once again claimed he
had declassified the documents.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.185
Trump claimed declassification procedures

00:14:45.185 --> 00:14:46.680
were essentially optional.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.048
telling Sean Hannity that as president,

00:14:48.048 --> 00:14:51.720
he had the power of
declassification with his mind.

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:53.340
- If you're the president
of the United States,

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:56.340
you can declassify just by
saying it's declassified,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:57.405
even by thinking about it.

00:14:57.405 --> 00:14:59.040
- And legal experts weighed in

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:00.720
and it should come as no surprise that no,

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:03.390
the declassification
processes are not optional

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:06.480
and presidents cannot
declassify them with their mind.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.458
And in addition to producing

00:15:07.458 --> 00:15:10.770
a new psychic presidential
power to Hannity's audience,

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.990
Trump also speculated
the FBI seized documents

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:14.790
from Mar-a-Lago because they were looking

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:17.190
for Hillary Clinton's deleted emails.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.440
- Were they looking for
the Hillary Clinton emails

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.470
that were deleted, but
they are around someplace?

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.360
- Yeah, I also don't know

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.310
how that makes any sense whatsoever.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:27.630
But it's not hopeless
for all those lawyers

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.340
